    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 041931\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n1231 PM MST Fri Sep 4 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Residual moisture may spark a few showers and storms this \n  afternoon, mainly south and east of Phoenix.\n\n- The moisture axis and corresponding rain chances will shift west into\n  southeast California and southwest Arizona during the weekend. \n\n- Temperatures will remain near to slightly below normal through this\n  weekend before warming to slightly above normal by early next \n  week. \n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH SUNDAY/... \nThe overall synoptic pattern continues to be defined by an elongated \nridge of high pressure extending from the Southern Plains into \nthe southeastern U.S. while a broad trough encompasses much of the\nPacific Northwest and west coast. Due to this pattern \nconfiguration, the Desert Southwest remains under dry southwesterly\nflow aloft. Residual low-level moisture from Hurricane Marie, \ncentered well to the west of the southern tip of the Baja \nPeninsula, remains across much south-central and southeast AZ \nwith dewpoints in the 60s while much drier air with dewpoints in \nthe 30s and 40s encompasses much of southeast CA and southwest AZ.\nThe residual moisture will still combine with strong daytime \nheating to cause cumulus buildups heading into this afternoon \nacross south-central AZ with the potential for a few showers and \nstorms to develop, mainly across the terrain escarpments. However,\nas the afternoon progresses, drier air will be filtering in from \nthe west, which will erode the low-level moisture somewhat and \nthus reduce instability significantly with the latest forecast \nsoundings from Phoenix showing CAPE values dropping from over 1000\nJ/KG earlier today to less than 100-200 J/KG by this afternoon. \nTherefore, any convective cells that attempt to develop this afternoon\nwill be very short-lived. \n\nAs Hurricane Marie continues to move northwestward well offshore \nfrom the Baja Peninsula over the weekend, some additional moisture \nwill be transported northward but this time towards southern CA and \nsouthwest AZ as PWATs rise to around 1.6-1.8\". Therefore, rain \nchances heading into this weekend will be mainly confined across \nthese areas with PoPs only topping out between 10-20%. An abundance \nof mid to upper-level moisture will result in extensive mid-high \nclouds across much of the area so as a result mid-level lapse rates \nand thus instability will be very poor with forecast soundings \nshowing little to no CAPE. As a result, any rainfall activity that \nmaterializes will be on the light side with little to no impacts. In \nfact, much of the activity that falls on Saturday across southeast \nCA and southwest AZ may actually be virga given that the low- \nlevels will remain very dry as dewpoints will still be in the 30s \nand 40s. Better low-level moisture is expected heading into \nSunday, however, the better forcing for ascent from an upper- \nlevel jet streak is likely to shift northward towards the Mohave \nDesert Region, where the best rain chances will be located. Fairly\nquiet weather is expected this weekend across much of south- \ncentral AZ as the lack of instability and forcing for ascent will \nhinder storm activity. \n\nThe continued presence of troughing to our west will continue to \nmaintain temperatures near to slightly below normal levels through\nthe weekend with highs generally in the upper 90s to low 100s, \nwith Saturday expected to be the coolest day. In fact, due to the \npresence of abundant mid to high clouds across much of the western\ndeserts on Saturday, temperatures may struggle to reach the \ntriple digits. The associated height falls from the troughing to \nour west will also result in another round of afternoon/early \nevening breeziness today across much of southeast CA and southwest\nAZ with peak gusts around 20-30 mph, potentially reaching up to \n40 mph in the typical wind prone areas of far southwest Imperial \nCounty. Slightly weaker winds are anticipated during the weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /MONDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/... \nDeterministic and ensemble guidance continue to differ on the \nmagnitude and positioning of the subtropical ridge early next week\nwhich will dictate how hot temperatures get and how soon rain \nchances will return to our area. Both the EPS and GEFS show the \nridge strengthening over the Southern Plains through the middle of\nthe week which will allow hghts aloft to increase across much of \nthe Desert Southwest. In turn, temperatures will also increase \nfrom near normal Monday to slightly above normal on Tuesday. The \ntemperature forecast becomes much more unclear heading into the \nmiddle of next week with large spreads in the NBM interquartile \nrange. This is likely due to differences in the magnitude of the \nridge with many EPS members showing a stronger ridge with 500 mb \nhghts approaching 596-598 dam while the GEFS shows slightly lower \noverall hghts. For now NBM temperatures show a cooling trend after\nTuesday which is likely due to an increase in moisture as \nsoutheasterly flow aloft returns to the lower deserts. \n\nPWATs are expected to increase back to around 1.5-1.8\" by the \nmiddle of next week which will bring rain chances back into the \npicture by the Wednesday-Thursday timeframe when PoPs increase to \naround 20-40% areawide. The GFS is even showing an easterly \nwave/inverted trough arriving into southern AZ along with \nadditional tropical Pacific moisture on Wednesday which could \nboost rain chances significantly, but we will likely have to wait \nfor more run to run consistency as these features are always \ndifficult to resolve at longer time scales. Either way, most of \nthe GEFS and EPS members are showing measurable precipitation \nacross portions of the area by the middle of next week.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1826Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT: \nWinds will follow a similar pattern to yesterday, with a SSE-SSW\ndirection for most of the day and a veering trend to W-SW this\nevening. Wind speeds will mostly be in the 5-10 kt range with\noccasional gusts into the teens. Scattered cumulus, based around\n10K ft AGL, will develop through this afternoon with a very low\nchance (5-10%) for a brief isolated shower or storm late this\nafternoon and evening. Light E'rly winds will eventually develop\nafter midnight tonight and then a S'rly component will develop\nagain by midday Saturday, but a quicker more pronounced W'rly\nshift is anticipated Saturday afternoon. SCT high clouds will\noverspread the area Saturday.\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH: \nAfternoon through early evening gusty winds will be the main\nweather impact through the TAF period. Gusts 20-25kt will be\ncommon at both terminals, from the S-SW at KBLH this afternoon and\nfrom the W-SW at KIPL this evening. Speeds will relax overnight\nand early morning where brief periods of nearly calm conditions\nwill exist. Mostly clear skies will prevail though this evening\nbefore mid and high clouds overspread the area heading into\nSaturday.\n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nResidual moisture may spark a few showers and storms this \nafternoon across south-central AZ. Rain chances will expand \nwestward through this weekend, becoming focused over the western \ndistricts Saturday and Sunday. However, the chances for wetting \nrains will remain very low at less than 10%. Afternoon minimum \nhumidities will range from 7-15% across southeast CA and between \n20-35% for the the AZ deserts today and will likely increase above\n20-25% areawide starting this weekend. Overnight recoveries will \nrange from poor across the western deserts to good across the \neastern districts tonight and increase to good areawide this \nweekend. Winds will remain elevated out of the south to southwest,\nwith the strongest gusts in Southeast CA and along the Lower \nColorado River Valley peaking between 20-30 mph this \nafternoon/early evening. Lighter winds, generally around 15 mph or\nless will transpire this weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Lojero\nLONG TERM...Salerno \nAVIATION...Benedict \nFIRE WEATHER...Salerno/Lojero\n",
